title: Letting a signal through
mode: draw
chapter: Choosing
check: behaves
parts: and or not
---- intro
An AND gate can be a **gate** in the everyday sense: a door that lets a signal through or holds it back.

Wire a signal `d` into one input and a control `en` (enable) into the other. When `en` is 1, the output is whatever `d` is. When `en` is 0, the output is 0, whatever `d` does.

| en | y |
|----|---|
| 0 | 0 |
| 1 | `d` |
---- task
Let `d` through to `y` only while `en` is on.
---- start
input d 0 0
input en 0 70
led y 300 30
---- reference
input d, en
led y
y = d & en
---- answer
input d 0 0
input en 0 70
and g1 140 20
led y 300 30
d -> g1.0
en -> g1.1
g1 -> y
---- hint1
One gate is enough. Which one gives 0 whenever one input is 0?
---- hint2
An AND gate, with `d` in one input and `en` in the other.
---- solution
`d` AND `en`: with `en` off the AND gives 0; with `en` on it gives `d`.
---- done
This is the idea behind every chooser in this chapter: let one signal through and hold the others back.
